# The national unit for britain. Items take n=0; the world items reviewed here change by level.
# Dates are in engine/notation/chronicle.ts with their sources.
lesson history-g5-britain-our-country-in-its-time v=1 format=teach grade=5 unit=9 subject=history nation=britain levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "England from Alfred to the Domesday Book"
  goal "Put England's story from Alfred to the Domesday Book in order, and say why a king wanted to know what everyone owned."

  look {
    scene 36x9 {
      timeline line from=850 to=1100 step=50 years=[871, 927, 1086] labels=["Alfred becomes king", "First king of England", "Domesday Book"] at=canvas(0, 0)
    }
    say """
      Alfred became king of the West Saxons in 871. His grandson Aethelstan became the first king of all England in 927. In 1066 William of Normandy took the throne, and in 1085 he sent men to every shire to write down who held the land, and how many ploughs, mills and animals there were. The book of their answers, finished in 1086, is the Domesday Book, and it still exists.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked history.n5-what n=0
    }
    show history.n5-order n=0 k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show history.n5-between n=0 k=0 hard-k=1
    show history.n5-century n=0 k=0 hard-k=1
    show history.voyage-order k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.continent-letter k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.print-order k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.n5-order n=0 k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=0
    level medium hard {
      show history.n5-what n=0
    }
    level easy {
      show history.n5-century n=0 k=1
    }
    level hard {
      show history.voyage-order k=5
    }
  }
  story {
    show history.n5-between n=0 k=1 hard-k=0
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show history.n5-why n=0
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show history.n5-find-out n=0
  }
  remember {
    say "Every date in our country's story can be put on the line and counted from, and every one came from a source."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is to use the grade's methods (timelines across year one, centuries, sources) on a
    chapter of the family's own country. The way in names what a thing was; the core orders three
    events, counts the years between two, names a century, and reviews voyages, continents and
    printing; the two-star question asks why something was done. If the child is stuck on
    counting, count to the next hundred first. The three-star task is for you to mark: a small
    piece of finding out with you. England's key stage 2 history asks for "the Viking and Anglo-Saxon struggle for the Kingdom of England to the time of Edward the Confessor", and the Domesday Book is where it ends.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level a worked example says what the thing was, the events come nearly in order, the years counted are the closest pair, and the review questions are the plainest."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the events are fully muddled, the years counted are the longer pair, the century asked has a trap, and the review questions cross year one."
  }
}
